WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.365
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.305 --> 00:00:08.720
PRESTON HOLMES: Thank
you, everyone, for coming.

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:10.240
My name is Preston Holmes.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:13.024
I'm part of our
IoT solutions team.

00:00:13.024 --> 00:00:14.440
And today, I'm
going to be talking

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:17.560
to sort of about a cornucopia
of a variety of sort

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:19.930
of performance-related
thoughts that

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:22.840
can go into your IoT solutions.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:25.900
So from the very
beginning, speed

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.710
has been part of
Google's philosophy.

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:31.570
And this was sort of published
in "10 Things We Know

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:33.530
To Be True" many years ago.

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:37.000
And in fact, back in
2009, we even began

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.440
running a number of experiments
of about, how does speed affect

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.280
the way people use Google?

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:47.080
So we would actually
inject intentional slowness

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:50.560
into Google search to just
a very random percentage

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:52.771
of users and measure,
how much were they still

00:00:52.771 --> 00:00:53.770
interacting with Google?

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:55.395
How many searches
were they doing.

00:00:55.395 --> 00:00:59.650
And we noticed just introducing
one to 400 milliseconds

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:03.670
of delay in Google search
decreased up to a half

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:06.490
a percent how many
searches people did.

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:08.860
And this idea of
very small amounts

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:11.770
of performance impacting the way
people interact with technology

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:13.360
also applies to e-commerce.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:16.000
So a large e-commerce
place did some research

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.820
and found that for every
100 milliseconds of latency

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:24.350
added to their experience, it
actually reduced sales by 1%.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:27.370
So speed definitely matters.

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:28.660
But what is speed?

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:32.590
When we say fast, it can be
an extremely relative concept.

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:35.590
So when we say something needs
to be fast, what do we mean?

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:38.320
Speed is measured through time.

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:40.630
Ranges from
nanoseconds to things

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:42.610
like weeks, months, and years.

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:45.940
So we have to think about
the context of time and speed

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:47.260
when we talk about performance.

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:50.680
So if we look at the very low
scale, less than a nanosecond.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:52.945
What are things that happen
less than a nanosecond?

00:01:52.945 --> 00:01:56.740
Well, it's the delay, the
time between each clock cycle

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:59.740
of like a three gigahertz CPU.

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:03.430
Or when you think about the
RF frequency and the vibration

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:07.840
of crystals emitting signals
for things like Wi-Fi--

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:12.850
less than a nanosecond
per frequency vibration.

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:16.220
And compared to something
like a microcontroller, which

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:18.370
you might find in an IoT
device, much slower, so

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:23.380
we have 125 nanoseconds between
each sort of rhythmic clock

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:26.200
cycle on something
like a eight-megahertz

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.146
microcontroller.

00:02:28.146 --> 00:02:29.770
So we go up now
talking about something

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:31.870
around 20 milliseconds.

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:34.930
When you talk about controlling
things like servos or dimming

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:37.340
lights, you have what's
called pulse width modulation.

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:40.420
This is when you change
the duration of a signal

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:43.000
out one of your I/O pins.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.470
And these typically are
about 20 milliseconds

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:48.830
in duration, these pulses.

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:51.200
Going to 50
milliseconds, this is

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:53.420
about the latency you're
going to find on the network

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:56.570
to reach your nearest
Google Cloud region overall.

00:02:56.570 --> 00:02:57.570
It can be a little less.

00:02:57.570 --> 00:02:58.250
It can be a little more.

00:02:58.250 --> 00:02:59.960
But if you're talking about
sort of within your region,

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:01.720
it could be around
50 milliseconds

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:04.975
at just the link
side of that timing.

00:03:04.975 --> 00:03:06.350
It's also about
the time it takes

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:10.220
for somebody tapping your
big toe to get to your brain.

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:13.310
We think of nerves and
sensation as being very fast,

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:16.760
but our nerves are actually
not that-- as sort of fast

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:18.440
as we think they are.

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:20.180
This is also about
the time you start

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:22.460
to perceive if you're watching
a video when somebody's

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:23.450
lip sync--

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:24.980
if the audio drifts
from the picture

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:27.540
is about 50 milliseconds.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:29.210
Now, if we go to a
farther Cloud Region,

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:31.460
if we're going halfway
around the world or something

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:35.110
like, that on a good connection,
it can be 250 milliseconds.

00:03:35.110 --> 00:03:37.520
So you can see that
this time scale

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:41.150
is sort of in our
long-distance networking frame.

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:43.910
It's also about the time
it takes to blink an eye.

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:45.950
So we often talk about
like in a blink of an eye

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:47.237
as being something very fast.

00:03:47.237 --> 00:03:49.070
But this is now something
we consider rather

00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:52.150
slow from a networking
point of view.

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:55.280
So we get into the kind of
next order of magnitude here.

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:57.080
BigQuery, while
it's very, very good

00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:02.150
at very fast queries across
very large data sets,

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:03.680
it does take one
to three seconds

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:08.170
to sort of plan a
query in BigQuery.

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:09.920
For those of you that
live near the ocean,

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:11.420
there's something
called slack tide,

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:14.570
which is sort of this delay as
the tide sloshes in and then

00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:15.209
out.

00:04:15.209 --> 00:04:16.250
That's called slack tide.

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:17.890
That lasts about an hour.

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:20.390
And finally, out to a month is
something that you might see.

00:04:20.390 --> 00:04:22.670
It takes about a
month for a strawberry

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:24.299
to go from flower to fruit.

00:04:24.299 --> 00:04:26.090
So if you're a strawberry
farmer and you're

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:28.190
trying to optimize
operations, you probably

00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:31.790
aren't caring about
10 nanoseconds of time

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:33.380
in the way the strawberry grows.

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:36.230
So every scale of time
has a different impact

00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:38.270
on performance.

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:40.430
Performance is also
greatly impacted

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:44.390
by many layers in your
stack, so everything

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:46.070
from the physics
of the equipment

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:48.480
you're using up to
the application layer.

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:49.970
So down at the
physics layer, this

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:52.490
might be something like
the thermal capacitance

00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:53.930
of a temperature sensor.

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:58.490
If you're looking for
100-millisecond latency

00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:00.410
on variation of
temperature, the truth

00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:04.130
is the temperature sensor
itself has thermal capacitance,

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:07.710
and you can't heated up
or cool it down that fast.

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:09.240
It has its own inertia.

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:12.050
So the physics can't give you
that kind of time resolution

00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:12.929
of temperature.

00:05:12.929 --> 00:05:15.470
On the device, you're going to
deal not only with things like

00:05:15.470 --> 00:05:18.759
the processor speed, but also if
you're using any sort of analog

00:05:18.759 --> 00:05:21.050
to digital converter-- we'll
talk about the performance

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:22.352
of those systems--

00:05:22.352 --> 00:05:23.810
you've got to send
this information

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:27.890
over a potential variety
of local and long-ranging

00:05:27.890 --> 00:05:29.030
communications protocols.

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:31.100
Each of those can
affect the performance.

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:34.310
Everything you do with the data,
ingesting it and processing it

00:05:34.310 --> 00:05:37.140
on the Cloud side, is obviously
going to have some impact.

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:38.660
And finally, there's
a set of things

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:40.970
around the way you can
develop your applications

00:05:40.970 --> 00:05:42.770
and the way performance
is perceived.

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:46.700
And that gets into more about
how we, as humans, can sort

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:48.120
of be fooled into performance.

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:50.760
So we'll talk a little
bit about each of these.

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:53.810
But before we go into them, we
need to know a little bit more

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:56.390
about how we measure
and communicate

00:05:56.390 --> 00:05:58.540
when we talk about performance.

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:00.800
And we're going to use this
analogy of moving water

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:02.340
from one place to another.

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:04.490
And there are really three
terms and three concepts

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:06.650
that are used when
measuring performance.

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:09.100
And that is operations,
latency, and throughput.

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:11.600
So operations is
essentially, how many times

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:15.470
can I do this task,
say, in an hour?

00:06:15.470 --> 00:06:17.330
The latency is, how
long does it take

00:06:17.330 --> 00:06:20.354
me to carry one bucket
from point A to point B?

00:06:20.354 --> 00:06:21.770
And throughput
often ends up being

00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:23.344
a bit of a combination
of the two,

00:06:23.344 --> 00:06:25.760
which is at the end of the
hour, how many gallons of water

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:27.140
have I moved?

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:31.470
Now, there's more than one
way to achieve throughput.

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:33.290
So you can take
three smaller buckets

00:06:33.290 --> 00:06:36.890
and move them in series, one
after another, very quickly

00:06:36.890 --> 00:06:38.510
with very low latency.

00:06:38.510 --> 00:06:40.640
Or you can move one
larger bucket more slowly,

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:43.160
and it results in
the same throughput.

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:45.710
You can move three
buckets concurrently.

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:50.510
And again, same latency in the
situation and same throughput.

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:55.560
Or you can combine combinations
of concurrency, latency, et

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:57.750
cetera to increase
your throughput,

00:06:57.750 --> 00:07:03.797
not necessarily by increasing
the size of each payload.

00:07:03.797 --> 00:07:05.880
Now, what's interesting
about these three measures

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:08.520
is how widely they
apply to a variety

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:10.140
of different
performance scenarios.

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:13.230
So this same measurement
is applied to how

00:07:13.230 --> 00:07:14.730
you measure disk operation.

00:07:14.730 --> 00:07:16.890
So operations, if
you hear of IOPS

00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:18.510
when you're talking
about hard drives

00:07:18.510 --> 00:07:21.870
or spinning disk versus SSD,
how many IOPS can a disk do,

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:23.350
that's operations.

00:07:23.350 --> 00:07:25.290
So this idea of
performance applies

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:27.150
to very small
local measurements,

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:30.210
such as between the CPU and
subsystems on the same board.

00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:31.950
But we can also think
of them as applying

00:07:31.950 --> 00:07:34.500
to how data moves
from the device

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:38.940
all the way to the application
that's running on the Cloud.

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:41.440
So first, let's start at
the lower layers here.

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:44.790
So fundamental to what,
in my mind defines,

00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:47.650
IoT is analog to digital.

00:07:47.650 --> 00:07:51.900
The world is full of all of this
infinitely resolution analog

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:52.470
information.

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:54.060
And the first step
in IoT is often

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:57.525
to digitize that into something
we can transmit on the wire.

00:07:57.525 --> 00:08:03.000
Now, analog signals are
inherently infinite resolution,

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:05.350
both in time and
in terms of value.

00:08:05.350 --> 00:08:08.100
So in x and y-axis, they're
essentially infinite

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:09.180
resolution.

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:12.080
And obviously, we don't
have infinite resolution

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:16.200
in computation, so the
analog-to-digital converter

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:20.460
quantiles that data and samples
it to turn it into binary data

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:21.630
that we can send.

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:24.980
And different analog-to-digital
converters, or ADCs,

00:08:24.980 --> 00:08:27.370
have different
performance capabilities.

00:08:27.370 --> 00:08:28.995
And the way you
measure the performance

00:08:28.995 --> 00:08:32.150
is in these two ways,
resolution and sample rate.

00:08:32.150 --> 00:08:34.679
So resolution is, how
many different values

00:08:34.679 --> 00:08:37.380
can you apply to that y-axis?

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:41.159
If you have, for
example, a seven-bit ADC,

00:08:41.159 --> 00:08:45.480
you can only get 127 values,
potentially, in your y-axis.

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:47.830
And if it's eight-bit,
you have 256.

00:08:47.830 --> 00:08:49.680
So that's your resolution.

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:52.320
And then the sampling rate
is, how many times per second

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:55.350
are you sampling
these vertical lines?

00:08:55.350 --> 00:08:57.480
How often are you
sampling the value?

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:00.150
And while those
sampling rates are often

00:09:00.150 --> 00:09:02.396
going to be very
high, and much higher

00:09:02.396 --> 00:09:04.770
than you're going to be sending
or transmitting telemetry

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:06.690
to the cloud, it's
important to know

00:09:06.690 --> 00:09:08.550
what the performance
of that sampling rate

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:12.362
is if you're trying to observe
subtle physical phenomenon

00:09:12.362 --> 00:09:14.070
that's happening at
very fine resolution,

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:16.830
either acoustic or
vibrational, something

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:18.720
subtle in your analog
signal that you

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:21.630
need to be able to capture and
then convert into something

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:24.750
you can send to the Cloud.

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:27.480
The other thing is to
think about time and clocks

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:28.110
on a device.

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:31.170
So clocks have one
set of time, which

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:33.810
is the relative time
of how many cycles

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:35.970
it's run since it booted.

00:09:35.970 --> 00:09:39.060
But you really need generally
some form of connection

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:40.980
to the real world time.

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:42.570
Right now, it's 12:44.

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:45.360
How does your device
know that it's 12:44?

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:47.340
And that's through
the real-time clock.

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:50.945
So these need to be set either
manually through a touch panel

00:09:50.945 --> 00:09:52.320
or through some
sort of protocol,

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:54.240
like Network Time Protocol.

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:55.800
They're usually
battery backed so

00:09:55.800 --> 00:09:58.170
that if the device
powers off and on again,

00:09:58.170 --> 00:09:59.730
it still has the current time.

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:01.990
It doesn't have to be reset.

00:10:01.990 --> 00:10:06.529
The quality of these real-time
clocks is measured as drift.

00:10:06.529 --> 00:10:08.820
And what's interesting, if
you've ever seen air quality

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:11.220
measurements in
parts per million,

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:13.170
drift in real-time
clocks is also

00:10:13.170 --> 00:10:14.920
measured in parts per million.

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:19.410
So 100 parts per million
drift in a measurement of,

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:22.590
how good is this clock, means
that in a million seconds,

00:10:22.590 --> 00:10:25.214
it will drift 100 seconds.

00:10:25.214 --> 00:10:26.880
And that's actually
eight seconds a day.

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:29.230
You can see how that could
kind of quickly build up.

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:31.140
So actually, most
real-time clocks

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:33.150
are far more accurate than that.

00:10:33.150 --> 00:10:37.250
And this matters because time
is used in a number of places

00:10:37.250 --> 00:10:38.520
in your IoT solution.

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:40.830
First, it's used in
the authentications.

00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:42.330
So you're not only
checking, say,

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:45.690
the validity of a
TLS certification

00:10:45.690 --> 00:10:47.070
when it might expire.

00:10:47.070 --> 00:10:51.150
But on IoT Core, we
enforce that the timing

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:54.390
of our authentication token
drift no more than 10 minutes

00:10:54.390 --> 00:10:56.550
outside of our server's time.

00:10:56.550 --> 00:10:59.500
So 10 minutes should cover
most real-time clock scenarios.

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:01.860
But if you're
outside of that, we

00:11:01.860 --> 00:11:05.280
won't recognize that
as a valid credential.

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:07.170
Further, you want
to have the time set

00:11:07.170 --> 00:11:10.650
across multiple devices in
your servers all to as close

00:11:10.650 --> 00:11:13.380
to the same time as you can
if you're trying to correlate

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:15.450
events across multiple devices.

00:11:15.450 --> 00:11:17.359
If one device is
set to one time,

00:11:17.359 --> 00:11:18.900
and another device
is set to another,

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:20.460
and you're trying
to understand how

00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:22.650
two events are
related in time, they

00:11:22.650 --> 00:11:25.620
need to share a common base.

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:28.440
And finally, if you're
looking at monitoring

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:30.450
the performance of
your system and you're

00:11:30.450 --> 00:11:33.450
trying to measure latency,
say, from the transmit time

00:11:33.450 --> 00:11:37.200
of a device to the Cloud, you
need a shared time across those

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:38.970
to accurately measure latencies.

00:11:38.970 --> 00:11:41.230
And we're going to
see that a bit later.

00:11:41.230 --> 00:11:42.210
So a quick tip--

00:11:42.210 --> 00:11:45.870
we offer very highly-accurate
free NTP service

00:11:45.870 --> 00:11:49.170
at time.google.com.

00:11:49.170 --> 00:11:50.430
Now, real-time is a phrase.

00:11:50.430 --> 00:11:52.904
We're going to see
this a couple of times.

00:11:52.904 --> 00:11:54.570
Real-time systems you
may have heard of.

00:11:54.570 --> 00:11:56.640
And one of the places
is, is it relates

00:11:56.640 --> 00:11:58.810
to the kind of
operating systems.

00:11:58.810 --> 00:12:02.700
So most of us are using a
CPU and a typical Linux-like

00:12:02.700 --> 00:12:05.400
operating system, where
application and system

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:09.210
processes all have access to
the lower layers of the hardware

00:12:09.210 --> 00:12:10.770
through a kernel.

00:12:10.770 --> 00:12:13.205
And with a kernel,
there are tasks

00:12:13.205 --> 00:12:14.830
that are running that
can be preempted.

00:12:14.830 --> 00:12:18.360
And the kernel can essentially
arbitrate which process

00:12:18.360 --> 00:12:20.340
gets access to the hardware.

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:23.490
And that's sometimes
not always advantageous

00:12:23.490 --> 00:12:24.500
when it comes to IoT.

00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:27.080
With a microcontroller,
the application

00:12:27.080 --> 00:12:30.379
can have high priority access
directly to the peripherals.

00:12:30.379 --> 00:12:31.920
And where that
matters is when you're

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:35.100
talking to hardware or
sensors or peripherals

00:12:35.100 --> 00:12:38.100
that have very strict
boundary timing

00:12:38.100 --> 00:12:40.260
on the-- say, the
durations of certain pulses

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:41.940
that need to be consistent.

00:12:41.940 --> 00:12:43.830
Your application
that's communicating

00:12:43.830 --> 00:12:46.650
on that kind of protocol
needs direct access

00:12:46.650 --> 00:12:49.630
to the peripherals
at essentially

00:12:49.630 --> 00:12:53.020
a potentially
microsecond-level accuracy.

00:12:53.020 --> 00:12:56.569
So when you hear about RTOSes,
or real-time operating systems,

00:12:56.569 --> 00:12:58.110
that generally means
that there's not

00:12:58.110 --> 00:13:00.900
a kernel in the middle that
could preempt you from getting

00:13:00.900 --> 00:13:04.550
access to the peripherals.

00:13:04.550 --> 00:13:07.002
So that's a bunch of
things on your devices.

00:13:07.002 --> 00:13:09.460
But what are some places that
Google invests at these lower

00:13:09.460 --> 00:13:12.550
layers of the actual
physical infrastructure that

00:13:12.550 --> 00:13:13.940
can affect your performance?

00:13:13.940 --> 00:13:16.130
And I've pulled out
just three of them.

00:13:16.130 --> 00:13:19.240
One of them is our investment
in the long lengths of fiber.

00:13:19.240 --> 00:13:22.630
Now, the speed of light
is about the fastest way

00:13:22.630 --> 00:13:24.400
we know to transmit information.

00:13:24.400 --> 00:13:28.570
I actually happen to work
alongside our quantum computing

00:13:28.570 --> 00:13:31.690
group, so potentially, they can
do quantum entanglement someday

00:13:31.690 --> 00:13:33.550
that could be somehow
instantaneous.

00:13:33.550 --> 00:13:34.550
But we're not there yet.

00:13:34.550 --> 00:13:36.850
So speed of light
is our best bet.

00:13:36.850 --> 00:13:38.279
Speed of light
through glass fiber

00:13:38.279 --> 00:13:40.570
is a little bit slower than
speed of light through air,

00:13:40.570 --> 00:13:41.986
but it's still the
best we can do.

00:13:41.986 --> 00:13:45.220
So we've invested heavily
in a lot of undersea fiber

00:13:45.220 --> 00:13:49.144
optic cables to make those
long jumps as fast as possible.

00:13:49.144 --> 00:13:50.560
Inside the data
center, we've also

00:13:50.560 --> 00:13:53.620
invested in a whole bunch of
software-defined networking

00:13:53.620 --> 00:13:57.490
and a set of cost-based
topology in our switches

00:13:57.490 --> 00:13:59.320
to make sure that
we have the most

00:13:59.320 --> 00:14:03.110
throughput between services
within our data center.

00:14:03.110 --> 00:14:06.100
And then finally, we've been
working on custom silicon

00:14:06.100 --> 00:14:09.366
to accelerate machine learning,
both training and inference.

00:14:09.366 --> 00:14:11.740
And as you may have heard just
in this morning's keynote,

00:14:11.740 --> 00:14:15.520
we've launched Edge TPU to
bring that kind of performance

00:14:15.520 --> 00:14:19.470
to machine learning out
onto the devices itself.

00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:21.770
So these are just three
areas where we're also

00:14:21.770 --> 00:14:24.080
investing in the actual
physical hardware

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:27.170
to increase performance.

00:14:27.170 --> 00:14:30.200
So now, we have our sort of
physical layers and hardware

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:31.910
layers a little bit covered.

00:14:31.910 --> 00:14:34.800
Let's talk about how
you package information.

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:36.950
So when you're
sending messages, you

00:14:36.950 --> 00:14:39.904
need to describe and
package a message.

00:14:39.904 --> 00:14:41.570
And the first thing
you want to consider

00:14:41.570 --> 00:14:43.236
is, how are you going
to serialize this?

00:14:43.236 --> 00:14:45.860
Are you going to use
JSON or something binary?

00:14:45.860 --> 00:14:48.890
And the way you encode a
message can impact how much time

00:14:48.890 --> 00:14:51.030
it takes on a device.

00:14:51.030 --> 00:14:53.000
It also impacts decoding
it on the Cloud side.

00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:55.083
But you might be more
concerned about the duration

00:14:55.083 --> 00:14:57.380
of time it takes on the device.

00:14:57.380 --> 00:14:59.720
How large the
resulting message is

00:14:59.720 --> 00:15:02.180
will affect how fast you
can send it over the wire,

00:15:02.180 --> 00:15:04.040
but it's also going
to affect the cost

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:07.859
of storing these messages as
they accumulate over time.

00:15:07.859 --> 00:15:10.400
And you need to consider whether
these messages are important

00:15:10.400 --> 00:15:13.594
to you more from a
latency point of view

00:15:13.594 --> 00:15:15.260
or more from a
throughput point of view.

00:15:15.260 --> 00:15:17.010
All of these can
affect the decisions

00:15:17.010 --> 00:15:19.300
around how you
describe the messages.

00:15:19.300 --> 00:15:22.700
The serialization is going
to decide also the format,

00:15:22.700 --> 00:15:26.209
whether or not you need to
have a schema, or description,

00:15:26.209 --> 00:15:27.500
of your message on either side.

00:15:27.500 --> 00:15:30.260
So some formats are
self-describing and more

00:15:30.260 --> 00:15:31.940
schema-less, like JSON.

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:34.760
Others, like protocol
buffers, require that each--

00:15:34.760 --> 00:15:36.680
the sender and the
receiver know in advance

00:15:36.680 --> 00:15:40.400
the structure, or the
schema, of the message.

00:15:40.400 --> 00:15:42.380
So Uber took quite
a long look at this,

00:15:42.380 --> 00:15:46.850
and they compared across several
different serialization formats

00:15:46.850 --> 00:15:50.120
and binary and
compression formats.

00:15:50.120 --> 00:15:51.750
And while you can't
read all these,

00:15:51.750 --> 00:15:55.220
essentially, the y-axis
is, how much time

00:15:55.220 --> 00:15:59.720
did it take to encode
and decode a given type?

00:15:59.720 --> 00:16:04.740
And the x-axis is, what is the
size of the resulting payload?

00:16:04.740 --> 00:16:08.980
And so the lower left
is sort of the best.

00:16:08.980 --> 00:16:10.940
It took the least
amount of time,

00:16:10.940 --> 00:16:13.340
and it resulted in
the smallest message.

00:16:13.340 --> 00:16:15.980
And so you'll see things like
protocol buffers and zlib

00:16:15.980 --> 00:16:18.080
compression and
thrift down here,

00:16:18.080 --> 00:16:20.450
whereas JSON is way out here.

00:16:20.450 --> 00:16:23.060
So while JSON's very
comfortable and familiar to us,

00:16:23.060 --> 00:16:25.520
it's not necessarily always
the most efficient way

00:16:25.520 --> 00:16:27.530
to send messages.

00:16:27.530 --> 00:16:29.420
Now, moving up,
there's going to be--

00:16:29.420 --> 00:16:32.040
this is a very rapid
and high-density talk.

00:16:32.040 --> 00:16:33.290
Hope you guys appreciate that.

00:16:33.290 --> 00:16:35.180
So as we go now,
we're sending messages

00:16:35.180 --> 00:16:37.460
over some sort of
a transport layer.

00:16:37.460 --> 00:16:41.390
There's a lot of these different
protocols of communication

00:16:41.390 --> 00:16:41.890
out there.

00:16:41.890 --> 00:16:42.931
And let's quickly review.

00:16:42.931 --> 00:16:45.050
On the right hand side
are sort of layers

00:16:45.050 --> 00:16:48.031
one through four of the
OSI layering here, which is

00:16:48.031 --> 00:16:49.280
that the layer one is really--

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:52.040
I think of it is that
the wires and waves,

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:55.410
how things move as currents or
radio waves through the air.

00:16:55.410 --> 00:16:57.590
Layer two is how you sort
of frame the messages

00:16:57.590 --> 00:16:59.254
on that carrier signal.

00:16:59.254 --> 00:17:00.920
And it's not until
layers three and four

00:17:00.920 --> 00:17:04.450
that we really get into
internet of things, IP protocol.

00:17:04.450 --> 00:17:07.730
However, there's a lot of
information in IoT solutions

00:17:07.730 --> 00:17:12.349
that spend some part of their
journey as non-IP traffic.

00:17:12.349 --> 00:17:15.869
And that might be over local
area connections or wider area

00:17:15.869 --> 00:17:16.369
connections.

00:17:16.369 --> 00:17:20.030
So locally, we have a
number of mesh protocols.

00:17:20.030 --> 00:17:22.099
So the performance
of, how many hops

00:17:22.099 --> 00:17:24.260
will that message make
on a mesh protocol,

00:17:24.260 --> 00:17:27.140
will affect your latency.

00:17:27.140 --> 00:17:30.650
Other protocols, which are
more legacy or industrial,

00:17:30.650 --> 00:17:33.850
are bound by the carrier signal.

00:17:33.850 --> 00:17:37.610
So modbus or other
RS45-type protocols, they're

00:17:37.610 --> 00:17:40.280
limited to how fast they
can transmit information

00:17:40.280 --> 00:17:42.800
by their very design.

00:17:42.800 --> 00:17:46.160
On the long-range side, you
have to consider whether you're

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:48.430
most concerned with
throughput, in which case, you

00:17:48.430 --> 00:17:51.020
might be looking more
for something like 5G,

00:17:51.020 --> 00:17:54.710
or cost and low bandwidth, which
you'd be looking at something

00:17:54.710 --> 00:17:57.350
like nb-iot or Sigfox or LoRA.

00:17:57.350 --> 00:17:59.570
So these are all
essentially different levers

00:17:59.570 --> 00:18:02.195
and knobs you have on the
transport layer to consider.

00:18:04.790 --> 00:18:07.190
The other one is to think
about the protocol you're

00:18:07.190 --> 00:18:08.610
sending over these transports.

00:18:08.610 --> 00:18:13.522
So IoT Core, we offer
both MQTT and HTTP.

00:18:13.522 --> 00:18:15.230
But there's going to
be some differences.

00:18:15.230 --> 00:18:17.360
And one of the biggest
ones is that MQTT

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:20.109
is a stateful protocol,
meaning you open a connection,

00:18:20.109 --> 00:18:21.650
and then, you can
use that connection

00:18:21.650 --> 00:18:23.150
for multiple messages.

00:18:23.150 --> 00:18:26.150
While HTTP is more
inherently stateless,

00:18:26.150 --> 00:18:28.570
and you need to set
up and tear down

00:18:28.570 --> 00:18:30.470
a connection for each
message you send.

00:18:30.470 --> 00:18:32.570
And you can see that
over time, that overhead

00:18:32.570 --> 00:18:34.760
of establishing
connections not only

00:18:34.760 --> 00:18:38.300
impact performance but
also power consumption.

00:18:38.300 --> 00:18:42.200
The other thing is that with
MQTT, since it's stateful,

00:18:42.200 --> 00:18:44.340
when you update a
device's configuration,

00:18:44.340 --> 00:18:47.300
it can be pushed essentially
immediately down to a device,

00:18:47.300 --> 00:18:50.240
whereas with MQTT, you
have to pull and see if you

00:18:50.240 --> 00:18:53.780
have any update config waiting.

00:18:53.780 --> 00:18:57.440
Furthermore, HTTP is
a text-based protocol.

00:18:57.440 --> 00:19:01.790
You cannot send binary
payloads directly over HTTP.

00:19:01.790 --> 00:19:03.860
You have to base64 encode them.

00:19:03.860 --> 00:19:05.870
That inflates the
size of the payload.

00:19:05.870 --> 00:19:09.430
You're going to pay a penalty on
the wire for those extra bytes

00:19:09.430 --> 00:19:10.930
of that base64 encoding.

00:19:14.090 --> 00:19:16.370
Inside a message, you
also need to think about,

00:19:16.370 --> 00:19:18.860
is there a way to economize
and batch information

00:19:18.860 --> 00:19:20.300
in a single message?

00:19:20.300 --> 00:19:23.720
So you might say that you
have many sensor readings,

00:19:23.720 --> 00:19:26.340
and they're all taken at
approximately the same time.

00:19:26.340 --> 00:19:28.730
Well, you can send one
time stamp and multiple

00:19:28.730 --> 00:19:31.430
readings in a single
message, and you

00:19:31.430 --> 00:19:34.140
have reduced the overhead
of your actual payload.

00:19:34.140 --> 00:19:37.790
Now, you have to balance that
benefit of combining messages

00:19:37.790 --> 00:19:40.040
into one message with
the fact that if you

00:19:40.040 --> 00:19:42.140
need to process
them independently,

00:19:42.140 --> 00:19:44.280
you may be paying
another sort of tax

00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:46.100
for that on the Cloud
side, because you're

00:19:46.100 --> 00:19:48.960
going to have to
unpack those messages.

00:19:48.960 --> 00:19:52.520
So the degree that you can gain
efficiency in the transmitting

00:19:52.520 --> 00:19:56.390
by packing more information
into a single message

00:19:56.390 --> 00:19:58.330
versus the unpacking
on the Cloud

00:19:58.330 --> 00:19:59.580
will be something to consider.

00:19:59.580 --> 00:20:01.430
But if you think about
all of the metadata

00:20:01.430 --> 00:20:03.620
about a message
that might be shared

00:20:03.620 --> 00:20:07.730
across multiple messages, that
represents a set of overhead

00:20:07.730 --> 00:20:12.760
that you can economize on by
using a thicker message type.

00:20:15.770 --> 00:20:17.900
All right, so very
briefly, I think--

00:20:17.900 --> 00:20:23.400
how many people realize that
they're using HTTP/2 every day?

00:20:23.400 --> 00:20:25.220
So most people, I
don't think, recognize

00:20:25.220 --> 00:20:27.428
that there's been this sort
of radical transformation

00:20:27.428 --> 00:20:31.100
that's happened in HTTP land
over the last several years.

00:20:31.100 --> 00:20:32.840
This is largely led
by Google's efforts

00:20:32.840 --> 00:20:36.440
to really improve the efficiency
and way in the way web content

00:20:36.440 --> 00:20:37.650
is delivered.

00:20:37.650 --> 00:20:39.890
So it used to be that
you'd open a web page.

00:20:39.890 --> 00:20:41.780
The web page would
load a bunch of images

00:20:41.780 --> 00:20:42.990
and pull down a bunch of CSS.

00:20:42.990 --> 00:20:45.290
And each of those was
another HTTP connection.

00:20:45.290 --> 00:20:46.320
It was very inefficient.

00:20:46.320 --> 00:20:50.750
So we looked at sort of
redesigning the way HTTP works.

00:20:50.750 --> 00:20:53.630
Now, we open something
much more like a stream

00:20:53.630 --> 00:20:58.820
that we can pipeline multiplex,
multiple pieces of information

00:20:58.820 --> 00:21:00.320
over the same channel.

00:21:00.320 --> 00:21:02.750
We deal with flow control,
so no head of line

00:21:02.750 --> 00:21:04.400
blocking, things like that.

00:21:04.400 --> 00:21:06.450
And we can push messages down.

00:21:06.450 --> 00:21:10.070
And this has really improved
the efficiency of the web.

00:21:10.070 --> 00:21:11.990
We've gone one step
further, and we also

00:21:11.990 --> 00:21:17.930
have an open RPC protocol that
pushes our sort of API methods

00:21:17.930 --> 00:21:21.709
over HTTP/2, and
that's called gRPC.

00:21:21.709 --> 00:21:23.750
There's a whole bunch
about gRPC I won't go into,

00:21:23.750 --> 00:21:25.208
but essentially,
it also allows you

00:21:25.208 --> 00:21:28.370
to find services and clients
in multiple languages.

00:21:28.370 --> 00:21:32.870
And this all rides over
the transport of HTTP/2.

00:21:32.870 --> 00:21:36.650
So IoT Core today delivers a lot
of its telemetry-- essentially,

00:21:36.650 --> 00:21:38.930
all of its telemetry
over a Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:21:38.930 --> 00:21:43.190
And Cloud Pub/Sub has been
an early and avid supporter

00:21:43.190 --> 00:21:45.990
of gRPC as a protocol.

00:21:45.990 --> 00:21:49.310
However, Pub/Sub isn't
something that is as simple

00:21:49.310 --> 00:21:50.137
as you might think.

00:21:50.137 --> 00:21:52.220
You'd sort of imagine, oh
yeah, publish a message,

00:21:52.220 --> 00:21:55.100
and that subscriber just
simply gets the message.

00:21:55.100 --> 00:21:56.450
And that's it.

00:21:56.450 --> 00:21:59.840
But there's a lot of knobs
and decisions available to you

00:21:59.840 --> 00:22:01.535
about tuning the
performance of Pub/Sub,

00:22:01.535 --> 00:22:04.370
and the first is whether
you do push or pull.

00:22:04.370 --> 00:22:07.430
Now push, you have
this overhead,

00:22:07.430 --> 00:22:10.070
as I was describing with
connection of, one message

00:22:10.070 --> 00:22:12.300
at a time to an HTTP endpoint.

00:22:12.300 --> 00:22:14.240
It's not necessarily
a very efficient way

00:22:14.240 --> 00:22:17.390
of getting a lot of
throughput out of Pub/Sub.

00:22:17.390 --> 00:22:20.900
We now support, through gRPC,
streaming or async pull,

00:22:20.900 --> 00:22:24.200
in which you can establish
a connection from a client

00:22:24.200 --> 00:22:27.590
and do streaming
messages from Pub/Sub.

00:22:27.590 --> 00:22:30.290
And this allows you to
attain much higher throughput

00:22:30.290 --> 00:22:34.034
than you would be from even
batch pulling messages.

00:22:34.034 --> 00:22:36.200
So this is something you
definitely want to look at.

00:22:36.200 --> 00:22:38.570
I think the takeaway
with performance

00:22:38.570 --> 00:22:41.510
and Pub/Sub so is
understand that if you

00:22:41.510 --> 00:22:43.820
try a naive or
simple measurement,

00:22:43.820 --> 00:22:47.300
you might need to do some tuning
to get the optimum throughput

00:22:47.300 --> 00:22:49.790
out of Pub/Sub.

00:22:49.790 --> 00:22:53.600
And one of those things is
we'll parallelize consumption.

00:22:53.600 --> 00:22:56.639
So you can do streaming
pull from a single thread,

00:22:56.639 --> 00:22:58.430
but then you can also
have multiple threads

00:22:58.430 --> 00:23:00.515
or multiple processes
doing streaming pull.

00:23:00.515 --> 00:23:03.740
And all of that will
increase throughput.

00:23:03.740 --> 00:23:06.140
The other type of way
in which parallelization

00:23:06.140 --> 00:23:08.690
can happen on Cloud is if you're
using Cloud Functions, Cloud

00:23:08.690 --> 00:23:10.400
Functions themselves
will scale out.

00:23:10.400 --> 00:23:12.890
Each function can
run concurrently

00:23:12.890 --> 00:23:15.720
across many instances.

00:23:15.720 --> 00:23:17.870
And finally, you can
use parallelization

00:23:17.870 --> 00:23:19.150
in your data processing.

00:23:19.150 --> 00:23:20.720
So now, we're
moving up the stack

00:23:20.720 --> 00:23:23.790
now into sort of data
processing, data management.

00:23:23.790 --> 00:23:26.810
So you can use techniques
such as MapReduce and Apache

00:23:26.810 --> 00:23:29.600
Beam and Dataflow to
process data in parallel

00:23:29.600 --> 00:23:32.330
that will get you through
your data processing tasks

00:23:32.330 --> 00:23:33.650
much faster.

00:23:33.650 --> 00:23:35.202
And in fact, in
Dataflow, there's

00:23:35.202 --> 00:23:37.160
one particular thing I
want to kind of call out

00:23:37.160 --> 00:23:38.701
which is pretty
interesting, and that

00:23:38.701 --> 00:23:40.970
is if anyone's worked with
data processing systems,

00:23:40.970 --> 00:23:43.370
like MapReduce,
the first task is

00:23:43.370 --> 00:23:47.220
that you divide a huge mountain
of work up into smaller chunks.

00:23:47.220 --> 00:23:50.600
And then, you divvy up all these
chunks to all these workers.

00:23:50.600 --> 00:23:53.510
The truth is that task
of divvying up work

00:23:53.510 --> 00:23:57.260
is not a very perfect
step in the process.

00:23:57.260 --> 00:23:58.940
And some workers
end up with kind

00:23:58.940 --> 00:24:01.970
of a crummier batch of
work than other workers,

00:24:01.970 --> 00:24:03.810
and they take longer.

00:24:03.810 --> 00:24:07.760
And so your actual
job duration is really

00:24:07.760 --> 00:24:10.301
limited by the slowest
worker of your set.

00:24:10.301 --> 00:24:12.050
And you can see that
on the left hand side

00:24:12.050 --> 00:24:14.100
with these long-tooth combs.

00:24:14.100 --> 00:24:17.850
Those represent workers that
got more work than others.

00:24:17.850 --> 00:24:21.710
And so the job does not complete
until the last worker is done.

00:24:21.710 --> 00:24:25.280
With a feature called Dynamic
Work Rebalancing in Dataflow,

00:24:25.280 --> 00:24:28.040
this is actually
dynamically reapportioned

00:24:28.040 --> 00:24:31.830
to different workers mid-job
so that the whole job completes

00:24:31.830 --> 00:24:32.330
faster.

00:24:32.330 --> 00:24:35.690
And that's not only going to
be faster to sort of result.

00:24:35.690 --> 00:24:38.420
That's also less compute
time you pay for,

00:24:38.420 --> 00:24:41.510
because you're not paying for
idle workers who have completed

00:24:41.510 --> 00:24:44.880
their task early.

00:24:44.880 --> 00:24:47.040
Now, let's talk
about high data rate.

00:24:47.040 --> 00:24:49.720
Whenever we're talking
about very high data rates,

00:24:49.720 --> 00:24:51.980
there's a whole bunch
of things to consider.

00:24:51.980 --> 00:24:55.090
So first of all, what
is your requirement

00:24:55.090 --> 00:24:57.040
around the integrity
of your data?

00:24:57.040 --> 00:25:01.810
If you require very high
consistency and high integrity

00:25:01.810 --> 00:25:04.270
around every message,
you will need things

00:25:04.270 --> 00:25:06.910
like locks and transactions.

00:25:06.910 --> 00:25:09.520
Now, a number of database
products and storage systems

00:25:09.520 --> 00:25:11.230
will give you
transactions, but they all

00:25:11.230 --> 00:25:13.012
come with some
performance costs.

00:25:13.012 --> 00:25:14.470
So if you need
that, it's something

00:25:14.470 --> 00:25:18.190
you need to consider into
your performance budget.

00:25:18.190 --> 00:25:21.670
The other thing is
many storage products

00:25:21.670 --> 00:25:25.090
suffer from some degree of
hotspotting if you simply

00:25:25.090 --> 00:25:29.830
try to write all of your values
in a very narrow key space.

00:25:29.830 --> 00:25:33.010
So most distributed
systems use some form

00:25:33.010 --> 00:25:36.730
of distributed hash
charting to make sure

00:25:36.730 --> 00:25:40.360
that everything is spread out
across many back end nodes.

00:25:40.360 --> 00:25:43.900
And if your keys tend to
all land on the same node,

00:25:43.900 --> 00:25:46.810
that system will slow
down and get burdened.

00:25:46.810 --> 00:25:49.451
So if you're using things
like timestamps in your keys,

00:25:49.451 --> 00:25:50.950
you need to make
sure there's enough

00:25:50.950 --> 00:25:54.520
randomness in the
preceding part of the key

00:25:54.520 --> 00:25:57.250
so that you're not
converging on one system.

00:25:57.250 --> 00:26:00.859
And we'll see a demo of
this in just a moment.

00:26:00.859 --> 00:26:02.650
The other is if you're
willing to trade off

00:26:02.650 --> 00:26:04.066
some degree of
durability, there's

00:26:04.066 --> 00:26:06.580
really no beating the
performance of memory.

00:26:06.580 --> 00:26:08.252
I mean, nothing
is faster than RAM

00:26:08.252 --> 00:26:09.460
when it comes to computation.

00:26:09.460 --> 00:26:13.840
So if you're willing to expose
yourself to some risk of loss

00:26:13.840 --> 00:26:17.020
and some risk of a little
bit of reduced durability,

00:26:17.020 --> 00:26:21.400
you can support a much
higher rate of computation

00:26:21.400 --> 00:26:24.420
if you're only keeping
a value in memory.

00:26:24.420 --> 00:26:26.390
And that brings us
to rate limiting.

00:26:26.390 --> 00:26:28.570
So rate limiting
is something that's

00:26:28.570 --> 00:26:34.825
there to protect systems
against overuse or higher costs.

00:26:34.825 --> 00:26:36.700
It's something that you
should be considering

00:26:36.700 --> 00:26:38.200
in designing your
systems, that you

00:26:38.200 --> 00:26:42.070
should be rate limiting any
consumers of your services

00:26:42.070 --> 00:26:44.740
to protect the performance
of your system.

00:26:44.740 --> 00:26:48.070
But you also need to
consider, how capable is

00:26:48.070 --> 00:26:52.450
that system of handling rate
limiting and the back pressure

00:26:52.450 --> 00:26:53.200
you might provide?

00:26:53.200 --> 00:26:55.720
So if you're working
with a legacy system,

00:26:55.720 --> 00:26:57.520
it might be more
incumbent upon you

00:26:57.520 --> 00:27:01.570
to simply scale as best you can,
because that system may simply

00:27:01.570 --> 00:27:05.350
break if you return
a 429 rate exceeded.

00:27:05.350 --> 00:27:07.670
So if it can't handle it,
you might need to deal that.

00:27:07.670 --> 00:27:10.390
You also need to be able to
handle being rate limited,

00:27:10.390 --> 00:27:13.360
potentially, or be aware of
any rate limits of systems

00:27:13.360 --> 00:27:15.897
that you're relying on.

00:27:15.897 --> 00:27:17.480
But there are some
tricks around this.

00:27:17.480 --> 00:27:20.164
So I'm going to show a demo
of this quick solution.

00:27:20.164 --> 00:27:21.580
And the idea is
here, we're trying

00:27:21.580 --> 00:27:24.670
to write a counter
value into Cloud

00:27:24.670 --> 00:27:26.410
Firestore at a very high rate.

00:27:26.410 --> 00:27:29.710
Now, Cloud Firestore
has a rate limit

00:27:29.710 --> 00:27:33.160
per document of one write
per second per document.

00:27:33.160 --> 00:27:36.010
So if I'm trying to update
a counter in Firestore

00:27:36.010 --> 00:27:38.920
at a very high rate, the most
I can write into Firestore

00:27:38.920 --> 00:27:41.870
for that value is
once per second.

00:27:41.870 --> 00:27:43.690
So what I'm doing in
this solution here

00:27:43.690 --> 00:27:47.590
is I'm using Redis,
an in-memory counter,

00:27:47.590 --> 00:27:50.740
and I'm updating that in-memory
counter at very high rates.

00:27:50.740 --> 00:27:54.250
And then, I'm using another
rate limiter attached to that

00:27:54.250 --> 00:27:57.290
and writing into Firestore
once every couple seconds.

00:27:57.290 --> 00:28:02.530
So let's see if I can
do a quick demo of this.

00:28:02.530 --> 00:28:06.580
So here, we have Cloud Firestore
and a very simple layout where

00:28:06.580 --> 00:28:09.960
we have one collection, Demo;
one document called Counter;

00:28:09.960 --> 00:28:11.740
and one field called Count.

00:28:15.560 --> 00:28:17.930
What I'm going to do is run
a little kind of load tester

00:28:17.930 --> 00:28:19.860
here.

00:28:19.860 --> 00:28:22.877
And what this is going to do
is hammer my Cloud Function.

00:28:22.877 --> 00:28:24.710
So you're going to see
this Cloud Function's

00:28:24.710 --> 00:28:27.620
probably gone a bit quiescent.

00:28:27.620 --> 00:28:29.720
The counter will start
up a little slow.

00:28:29.720 --> 00:28:34.309
But using a rate of 200 requests
per second for 80 seconds,

00:28:34.309 --> 00:28:35.850
I'm going to hit
this Cloud Function.

00:28:41.410 --> 00:28:42.290
I might refresh.

00:28:42.290 --> 00:28:43.140
So it did work.

00:28:43.140 --> 00:28:45.639
Sometimes, you've got to reload
this dashboard if it's lost.

00:28:45.639 --> 00:28:48.090
So you're going to see
this value of the count

00:28:48.090 --> 00:28:50.530
go up as the Cloud
Function warms up.

00:28:50.530 --> 00:28:53.217
You'll see this increase
every few seconds.

00:28:53.217 --> 00:28:55.050
But what's happening
is Redis is maintaining

00:28:55.050 --> 00:28:57.510
a high-speed counter
in memory and then,

00:28:57.510 --> 00:29:00.350
every couple of seconds,
writing the current count

00:29:00.350 --> 00:29:01.170
into Firestore.

00:29:01.170 --> 00:29:03.570
So this is a rate of
increase that you could never

00:29:03.570 --> 00:29:07.572
put directly into Firestore.

00:29:07.572 --> 00:29:09.780
If you tried to write every
increment of this counter

00:29:09.780 --> 00:29:13.180
into Firestore, you'd just start
getting errors from Firestore,

00:29:13.180 --> 00:29:15.790
because it's protecting
itself with the rate limiting.

00:29:15.790 --> 00:29:17.580
So this is kind
of a technique you

00:29:17.580 --> 00:29:20.430
can use to balance performance
of different layers

00:29:20.430 --> 00:29:25.380
of your system with some degree
of trade-off of throughput

00:29:25.380 --> 00:29:27.374
and rate and durability.

00:29:31.330 --> 00:29:34.870
All right, I very
quickly can kind of just

00:29:34.870 --> 00:29:37.660
walk you through a little
bit of what this looks like.

00:29:37.660 --> 00:29:40.390
We have a function
counter limit.

00:29:40.390 --> 00:29:43.775
It establishes a rate
limiter based on Redis.

00:29:43.775 --> 00:29:45.150
Actually, I have
a laser pointer.

00:29:45.150 --> 00:29:47.650
I can help you guys quickly.

00:29:47.650 --> 00:29:51.810
Here, the rate limiter
is established.

00:29:51.810 --> 00:29:54.280
I increment Redis here.

00:29:54.280 --> 00:29:57.070
No matter what, I can increment
Redis, because Redis basically

00:29:57.070 --> 00:29:59.830
has close to infinite counts--

00:29:59.830 --> 00:30:02.170
the ability, in the
system, at least.

00:30:02.170 --> 00:30:05.440
And then here, I check
whether my rate limiter

00:30:05.440 --> 00:30:07.060
has any available capacity.

00:30:07.060 --> 00:30:09.010
So rate limiting is
usually done in some form

00:30:09.010 --> 00:30:11.110
of where you have a certain
number of operations

00:30:11.110 --> 00:30:12.710
in a window of time.

00:30:12.710 --> 00:30:15.610
So I'm sort of setting a rate
limit of once every three

00:30:15.610 --> 00:30:17.140
seconds here.

00:30:17.140 --> 00:30:21.050
And then if I have available
capacity on my rate limiter,

00:30:21.050 --> 00:30:24.790
I will write out to Firestore,
write out the document.

00:30:24.790 --> 00:30:28.330
That's all the code for
what that demo essentially

00:30:28.330 --> 00:30:29.040
does right there.

00:30:33.440 --> 00:30:36.910
So now, we're up into
the application layer,

00:30:36.910 --> 00:30:41.105
so a couple points, a couple of
slides here on the application.

00:30:41.105 --> 00:30:43.480
So the first thing you can do
is when you're dealing with

00:30:43.480 --> 00:30:45.880
different types of data--
and say it's time series--

00:30:45.880 --> 00:30:49.340
if you're collecting data,
say, from every 30 seconds,

00:30:49.340 --> 00:30:51.760
but most of your users are
interested in something that's

00:30:51.760 --> 00:30:55.420
an average of an hour, you don't
need to compute that average

00:30:55.420 --> 00:30:57.400
for every user for every view.

00:30:57.400 --> 00:31:00.110
You should be pre-computing
certain information

00:31:00.110 --> 00:31:01.720
that people are
going to be working

00:31:01.720 --> 00:31:03.480
with in the application.

00:31:03.480 --> 00:31:06.064
There is a wide range of
caching techniques out there.

00:31:06.064 --> 00:31:07.480
I'm not going to
go into them all.

00:31:07.480 --> 00:31:10.690
But caching is an important
part around the performance

00:31:10.690 --> 00:31:11.600
of your application.

00:31:11.600 --> 00:31:14.980
If there's certain data you
know everybody is looking for,

00:31:14.980 --> 00:31:17.410
have that available
in a cache somewhere,

00:31:17.410 --> 00:31:19.520
potentially even at
the presentation layer.

00:31:19.520 --> 00:31:23.590
So I'm a big believer still in
the power of web applications.

00:31:23.590 --> 00:31:26.800
And web applications are
becoming more and more capable.

00:31:26.800 --> 00:31:28.630
In the browser,
in the client, you

00:31:28.630 --> 00:31:33.160
can do quite a bit in terms
of pre-computing information

00:31:33.160 --> 00:31:34.750
in your local
application and then

00:31:34.750 --> 00:31:37.990
sending back to Cloud
something every few moments.

00:31:37.990 --> 00:31:40.690
If somebody is dragging
a slider, for example,

00:31:40.690 --> 00:31:45.337
you don't need to send
every loop of the browser.

00:31:45.337 --> 00:31:47.170
You don't need to send
a value to the Cloud.

00:31:47.170 --> 00:31:49.590
You might do it only once
the slider is released,

00:31:49.590 --> 00:31:51.890
that kind of thing.

00:31:51.890 --> 00:31:54.020
You can be proactive,
and this can really

00:31:54.020 --> 00:31:56.900
improve the perceived
performance of an application.

00:31:56.900 --> 00:32:00.470
So again, in Firestore, it has
a system called Firestore watch.

00:32:00.470 --> 00:32:03.260
That allows all of the clients,
all of the applications

00:32:03.260 --> 00:32:05.510
that are interested in
the piece of information

00:32:05.510 --> 00:32:07.580
to watch for any change.

00:32:07.580 --> 00:32:09.890
When that value has
changed in the database,

00:32:09.890 --> 00:32:12.560
it's immediately pushed
out to all the clients.

00:32:12.560 --> 00:32:14.960
So there is no need
to write a polling

00:32:14.960 --> 00:32:17.000
loop in your application
if that information is

00:32:17.000 --> 00:32:18.830
pushed immediately.

00:32:18.830 --> 00:32:21.440
Firestore also has a feature
called latency compensation.

00:32:21.440 --> 00:32:23.420
So in complex
applications, you might

00:32:23.420 --> 00:32:26.810
have one piece of information
in the application that

00:32:26.810 --> 00:32:29.970
affects many other parts
of the application.

00:32:29.970 --> 00:32:33.710
And when you change a value
in the client in Firestore,

00:32:33.710 --> 00:32:37.640
it actually returns
the write immediately

00:32:37.640 --> 00:32:40.400
to the rest of the
application optimistically.

00:32:40.400 --> 00:32:43.100
So this is compensating for
latency to the back end.

00:32:43.100 --> 00:32:45.470
It then has metadata
that tells you,

00:32:45.470 --> 00:32:48.440
does this write actually
have a pending Cloud write,

00:32:48.440 --> 00:32:49.620
or is it completed?

00:32:49.620 --> 00:32:51.900
And so you can differentiate
that in your app.

00:32:51.900 --> 00:32:54.620
But you don't have to
build your app to assume--

00:32:54.620 --> 00:32:58.950
to wait for the long trip, round
trip for the-- to the response.

00:32:58.950 --> 00:33:01.050
The other thing is--

00:33:01.050 --> 00:33:03.440
again, remember when I talked
about the frame being--

00:33:03.440 --> 00:33:06.020
spanning from
nanoseconds to minutes--

00:33:06.020 --> 00:33:09.480
performance sometimes is about
minutes to hours to people

00:33:09.480 --> 00:33:09.980
right.

00:33:09.980 --> 00:33:13.340
If you use push notifications
in an application,

00:33:13.340 --> 00:33:15.500
you bring to
somebody's attention

00:33:15.500 --> 00:33:18.440
that there's something happening
they should care about.

00:33:18.440 --> 00:33:22.310
And that can get them back into
the application within minutes,

00:33:22.310 --> 00:33:24.350
as opposed to at
the end of the day,

00:33:24.350 --> 00:33:26.310
they think to check the
status of something.

00:33:26.310 --> 00:33:27.920
So push notifications
are another way

00:33:27.920 --> 00:33:31.100
of being proactive
with your users.

00:33:31.100 --> 00:33:33.230
And then finally
things that I put

00:33:33.230 --> 00:33:35.150
in this category of
suggested responses

00:33:35.150 --> 00:33:40.370
are we all use everyday
applications that are very

00:33:40.370 --> 00:33:42.050
helpful in auto-completing.

00:33:42.050 --> 00:33:44.270
If you go to the same
web page every day

00:33:44.270 --> 00:33:46.520
and you start to type
that URL in Chrome,

00:33:46.520 --> 00:33:48.890
it ranks it up there
right at the top.

00:33:48.890 --> 00:33:50.510
It knows which is most active.

00:33:50.510 --> 00:33:54.140
And that saves many, many
seconds of our interactions

00:33:54.140 --> 00:33:55.530
with applications every day.

00:33:55.530 --> 00:33:58.580
So think about how to be
proactive in suggesting

00:33:58.580 --> 00:34:01.580
responses at the
application layer.

00:34:01.580 --> 00:34:03.500
And finally,
offline-- all right,

00:34:03.500 --> 00:34:08.960
so this is something where
obviously as a Cloud platform,

00:34:08.960 --> 00:34:11.750
we're interested in the
application tying greatly

00:34:11.750 --> 00:34:13.909
to capabilities that
we offer on the Cloud.

00:34:13.909 --> 00:34:15.949
But we also recognize
that applications still

00:34:15.949 --> 00:34:18.480
need to function offline.

00:34:18.480 --> 00:34:21.560
So if we're thinking about
things in a browser--

00:34:21.560 --> 00:34:24.115
we have things that we've
really been investing recently

00:34:24.115 --> 00:34:26.270
at Google called progressive
web applications.

00:34:26.270 --> 00:34:29.389
It allows web applications
news local storage

00:34:29.389 --> 00:34:33.290
and web workers to maintain a
fully functioning experience

00:34:33.290 --> 00:34:36.150
while the machine's offline.

00:34:36.150 --> 00:34:38.810
Firestore also will
sync data locally

00:34:38.810 --> 00:34:41.210
to the local storage system
or the browser offline,

00:34:41.210 --> 00:34:44.330
so the application can
continue to function.

00:34:44.330 --> 00:34:45.920
I mentioned Edge TPU earlier.

00:34:45.920 --> 00:34:48.590
The idea of running
inference on the Edge

00:34:48.590 --> 00:34:51.440
means that if you have a
critical behavior and the use

00:34:51.440 --> 00:34:54.530
of ML on a device on
the Edge, that behavior

00:34:54.530 --> 00:34:56.874
needs to persist
and be functional,

00:34:56.874 --> 00:34:58.790
even if you've lost a
connection to the Cloud,

00:34:58.790 --> 00:35:01.170
so being able to
do ML on the Edge.

00:35:01.170 --> 00:35:02.900
And I would actually
argue that--

00:35:02.900 --> 00:35:05.640
be mindful about having
Cloud in the loop,

00:35:05.640 --> 00:35:08.630
in the application loop in
terms of the user's experience.

00:35:08.630 --> 00:35:10.130
The canonical idea
that you're going

00:35:10.130 --> 00:35:12.630
to flip a light switch
and the light turns on,

00:35:12.630 --> 00:35:16.100
it's probably not a best
place to put hard dependency

00:35:16.100 --> 00:35:18.920
of having Cloud in that loop.

00:35:18.920 --> 00:35:23.000
Have that loop happen locally
offline, and then async,

00:35:23.000 --> 00:35:25.850
keep that data synchronized,
or report it to the Cloud.

00:35:25.850 --> 00:35:29.750
But don't put a Cloud path
in a critical performance

00:35:29.750 --> 00:35:32.180
path on a local
application experience.

00:35:35.540 --> 00:35:39.020
So monitoring-- you cannot
improve the performance

00:35:39.020 --> 00:35:41.330
of something you're
not measuring.

00:35:41.330 --> 00:35:43.650
And there's a lot
you can be measuring.

00:35:43.650 --> 00:35:46.910
So you can be measuring
performance of the hardware,

00:35:46.910 --> 00:35:48.780
performance of the device.

00:35:48.780 --> 00:35:51.860
You can be measuring the
performance of application

00:35:51.860 --> 00:35:55.730
software either on the device
or at the presentation layer.

00:35:55.730 --> 00:35:58.550
You can be measuring
performance of the network,

00:35:58.550 --> 00:36:01.070
either something we provide
you on our part of the network

00:36:01.070 --> 00:36:03.380
or something that
you measure yourself

00:36:03.380 --> 00:36:09.170
from the end-to-end reality
of your device to a server.

00:36:09.170 --> 00:36:10.820
So it's very critical.

00:36:10.820 --> 00:36:15.380
It's almost nonsensical to talk
about knowing your performance

00:36:15.380 --> 00:36:18.140
or improving your performance
if you don't have measurements

00:36:18.140 --> 00:36:21.530
that you can tell whether
you've made an improvement.

00:36:21.530 --> 00:36:25.520
I'm going to show another
brief demo-- oh, all right--

00:36:25.520 --> 00:36:27.920
switched the order up on
myself here a little bit.

00:36:27.920 --> 00:36:30.180
When you're talking about
measurements of performance,

00:36:30.180 --> 00:36:31.638
it's really important
to understand

00:36:31.638 --> 00:36:34.670
that some measurements are a
lot more useful and valuable

00:36:34.670 --> 00:36:35.370
than others.

00:36:35.370 --> 00:36:40.760
And one of them that can be
not so helpful is an average.

00:36:40.760 --> 00:36:44.480
This is a quick graph of
two distributions of data

00:36:44.480 --> 00:36:47.210
that have the
exact same average,

00:36:47.210 --> 00:36:49.280
but obviously, they're
very different.

00:36:49.280 --> 00:36:53.480
If you were only measuring
an average for these two data

00:36:53.480 --> 00:36:57.480
sets, they would look identical
to your reporting system.

00:36:57.480 --> 00:37:00.510
You would say, oh, no,
we have a average of 100.

00:37:00.510 --> 00:37:01.730
It's great.

00:37:01.730 --> 00:37:04.940
But clearly, the distribution
of this data is very different.

00:37:04.940 --> 00:37:08.440
So what is common in
monitoring systems

00:37:08.440 --> 00:37:10.340
is to look at
percentiles or quantiles,

00:37:10.340 --> 00:37:13.070
where you're essentially looking
at buckets of information

00:37:13.070 --> 00:37:16.010
across a distribution,
and you're caring about,

00:37:16.010 --> 00:37:18.830
potentially, what are most
of our requests doing,

00:37:18.830 --> 00:37:23.010
or what is our lowest 10%
of all of our requests?

00:37:23.010 --> 00:37:26.320
So if you see numbers
like P99 or P95,

00:37:26.320 --> 00:37:28.800
or how many nines
of what your latency

00:37:28.800 --> 00:37:30.720
is at three 9s,
those kind of things,

00:37:30.720 --> 00:37:35.830
these are all dealing with
quantiles and percentiles.

00:37:35.830 --> 00:37:38.713
So back to the demo.

00:37:46.110 --> 00:37:47.325
Oh, no.

00:37:47.325 --> 00:37:49.345
I think my battery has died.

00:37:49.345 --> 00:37:50.580
So I have a backup.

00:37:50.580 --> 00:37:53.750
So I have a device
that's been publishing,

00:37:53.750 --> 00:37:58.840
except it has
tuckered out on me,

00:37:58.840 --> 00:38:02.000
so the backup here is
I have a screen shot.

00:38:13.063 --> 00:38:17.980
I'm gonna see if I can't
get this to come back online

00:38:17.980 --> 00:38:22.230
while I do this, but I'll go
through the screenshot first.

00:38:22.230 --> 00:38:23.850
So what we have here
in the screenshot

00:38:23.850 --> 00:38:28.190
is the measurement of the
publish acknowledgment

00:38:28.190 --> 00:38:32.870
latency over MQTT to IoT Core.

00:38:32.870 --> 00:38:36.720
And so this is a measurement
that we can't provide you

00:38:36.720 --> 00:38:38.250
on the Cloud side.

00:38:38.250 --> 00:38:42.270
This can only be observed
from the device side.

00:38:42.270 --> 00:38:49.592
So if a device is sending
a message over MQTT,

00:38:49.592 --> 00:38:51.300
we do a couple
interesting things for you

00:38:51.300 --> 00:38:53.350
on the performance side
and the durability side.

00:38:53.350 --> 00:38:56.790
So what we do is when we
take the message from MQTT

00:38:56.790 --> 00:39:00.540
and write it to Pub/Sub, we
will not acknowledge the message

00:39:00.540 --> 00:39:05.460
on MQTT until after it's been
written to Pub/Sub so that when

00:39:05.460 --> 00:39:08.460
you get an acknowledgment
on the MQTT protocol,

00:39:08.460 --> 00:39:12.420
you actually know that data has
already arrived safely and been

00:39:12.420 --> 00:39:15.260
delivered into Cloud
Pub/Sub, and Cloud Pub/Sub

00:39:15.260 --> 00:39:18.410
is a highly-durable,
highly-reliable system.

00:39:18.410 --> 00:39:20.910
But you might care about like,
well, how long did that take?

00:39:20.910 --> 00:39:23.970
From the device side, when
do I get acknowledgment

00:39:23.970 --> 00:39:25.320
on the device side?

00:39:25.320 --> 00:39:29.220
And because the network
is not a perfect system,

00:39:29.220 --> 00:39:32.830
not every message is going
to have identical latency.

00:39:32.830 --> 00:39:36.030
And what you want to know when
you're looking at the quantiles

00:39:36.030 --> 00:39:39.750
here is, what's the
average speed of my,

00:39:39.750 --> 00:39:41.910
like, best half of
all my messages.

00:39:41.910 --> 00:39:45.780
So that would be your 0.5 here.

00:39:45.780 --> 00:39:48.220
I'm going to see if I'm
lucky enough the demo gods--

00:39:48.220 --> 00:39:48.720
yeah.

00:39:48.720 --> 00:39:50.053
So I'm starting to get data now.

00:39:50.053 --> 00:39:53.700
I'm going after switch my time
resolution to the last five

00:39:53.700 --> 00:39:57.100
minutes to get
anything useful here.

00:39:57.100 --> 00:39:59.910
So you can see here that I
can look at that measurement

00:39:59.910 --> 00:40:02.370
and see, as I hover
over each of these,

00:40:02.370 --> 00:40:04.920
what the performance of
each of these measures

00:40:04.920 --> 00:40:07.050
is, what each of
these buckets is.

00:40:07.050 --> 00:40:13.020
And so if you see
a very high spread,

00:40:13.020 --> 00:40:14.520
that may or may
not matter to you.

00:40:14.520 --> 00:40:16.050
But if you have
a user who's sort

00:40:16.050 --> 00:40:18.300
of reporting, or somehow you're
trying to debug something--

00:40:18.300 --> 00:40:20.800
you're saying, wow, sometimes
something seems to take a long

00:40:20.800 --> 00:40:22.860
time; is it in my system--

00:40:22.860 --> 00:40:26.320
well, you may see something
like a 95th percentile that's

00:40:26.320 --> 00:40:27.192
slower.

00:40:27.192 --> 00:40:28.650
And that might be
fine, because you

00:40:28.650 --> 00:40:30.480
know that 95% of
your messages are

00:40:30.480 --> 00:40:34.810
getting through within
some faster rate than that.

00:40:34.810 --> 00:40:35.940
But that's good to know.

00:40:35.940 --> 00:40:40.271
And if you were simply measuring
your average latencies,

00:40:40.271 --> 00:40:41.020
you wouldn't know.

00:40:41.020 --> 00:40:42.670
You'd lose that information.

00:40:42.670 --> 00:40:46.020
You'd lose that insight
into, what is the range,

00:40:46.020 --> 00:40:48.210
or what is the
distribution of performance

00:40:48.210 --> 00:40:49.890
I'm seeing in these systems?

00:40:49.890 --> 00:40:51.550
You can do the
same kind of thing

00:40:51.550 --> 00:40:55.620
not only in this publish
acknowledgments speed on MQTT

00:40:55.620 --> 00:40:58.260
but at any other
leg of your system.

00:40:58.260 --> 00:41:01.290
And you can, in fact,
build distributed traces

00:41:01.290 --> 00:41:04.710
of these kind of things over
the whole end-to-end span.

00:41:04.710 --> 00:41:07.320
So I've pulled up here
also that there's--

00:41:07.320 --> 00:41:09.630
the idea of monitoring
not only applies

00:41:09.630 --> 00:41:13.170
to the sort of inward-looking
monitoring and measuring

00:41:13.170 --> 00:41:15.960
of information around the
performance of your system,

00:41:15.960 --> 00:41:19.930
but it can be externally-facing
through the sensors themselves.

00:41:19.930 --> 00:41:23.520
So the lower two graphs
here show the light level.

00:41:23.520 --> 00:41:25.570
And if I if I sort
pull out the device--

00:41:25.570 --> 00:41:29.160
now that it's restarted here,
it's got a little light sensor.

00:41:29.160 --> 00:41:30.540
It's got a little display.

00:41:30.540 --> 00:41:34.140
And you'll see that as it
gets into the stage lighting,

00:41:34.140 --> 00:41:37.610
this should tick
up significantly.

00:41:37.610 --> 00:41:39.660
And so sometimes, the
same kind of monitoring

00:41:39.660 --> 00:41:42.690
you're going to do might
be relevant for monitoring

00:41:42.690 --> 00:41:45.750
the performance of
your physical systems.

00:41:45.750 --> 00:41:47.670
So we've been talking
a lot about monitoring

00:41:47.670 --> 00:41:51.930
the performance of the
devices or the infrastructure

00:41:51.930 --> 00:41:52.530
of the Cloud.

00:41:52.530 --> 00:41:55.320
But if you're interested
in the performance of,

00:41:55.320 --> 00:41:57.440
say, a garage door--

00:41:57.440 --> 00:41:59.520
use a sort of trivial
example here--

00:41:59.520 --> 00:42:03.780
and saying, hey, you
know, the garage door is

00:42:03.780 --> 00:42:05.760
all of a sudden taking
a lot longer to open,

00:42:05.760 --> 00:42:08.710
or a furnace is taking
a lot longer to warm up,

00:42:08.710 --> 00:42:10.920
these are-- now, we're
talking about the performance

00:42:10.920 --> 00:42:13.890
of the actual
applications of IoT

00:42:13.890 --> 00:42:17.280
as opposed to the performance
of the infrastructure

00:42:17.280 --> 00:42:19.500
and the technology
supporting IoT.

00:42:19.500 --> 00:42:22.470
But the truth is it's
time series data,

00:42:22.470 --> 00:42:25.200
and it's data that is
operationally relevant to you.

00:42:25.200 --> 00:42:29.025
So the same tools can
apply relatively equally.

00:42:33.070 --> 00:42:37.230
So demo recovery there.

00:42:41.060 --> 00:42:46.170
All right, and then just a
quick comment about cost,

00:42:46.170 --> 00:42:50.840
because performance can often
be improved if you throw

00:42:50.840 --> 00:42:52.340
a lot of money at a problem.

00:42:52.340 --> 00:42:57.110
If you're worried about handling
huge surges of information,

00:42:57.110 --> 00:43:00.230
you can simply have a lot of
very expensive standby capacity

00:43:00.230 --> 00:43:02.270
ready to process it.

00:43:02.270 --> 00:43:03.830
If you want to
economize on cost,

00:43:03.830 --> 00:43:07.610
you may work-- you just may
let auto-scaling systems

00:43:07.610 --> 00:43:09.290
sort of scale out
and handle the load.

00:43:09.290 --> 00:43:10.831
One of the great
things about Pub/Sub

00:43:10.831 --> 00:43:14.720
is it has essentially
instantaneous elasticity

00:43:14.720 --> 00:43:15.950
on the publishing side.

00:43:15.950 --> 00:43:17.690
It'll act as that
surge tank right.

00:43:17.690 --> 00:43:21.010
So if you all of a sudden want
to publish 100,000 messages,

00:43:21.010 --> 00:43:22.100
you can do that.

00:43:22.100 --> 00:43:24.260
It might fill up a
queue, and you may not

00:43:24.260 --> 00:43:28.730
want to have 20 servers
standing by expensively waiting

00:43:28.730 --> 00:43:32.900
to process them all, but you've
at least handled the ingestion

00:43:32.900 --> 00:43:34.710
instantaneously.

00:43:34.710 --> 00:43:37.530
So certain things align
with performance and cost.

00:43:37.530 --> 00:43:38.540
Certain things are not.

00:43:38.540 --> 00:43:42.140
So IoT Core and Pub/Sub,
you really build on bytes.

00:43:42.140 --> 00:43:46.460
You are not build to have
a faster byte transmitted.

00:43:46.460 --> 00:43:48.470
So there really
isn't a extra cost

00:43:48.470 --> 00:43:51.980
associated with more
performance for things

00:43:51.980 --> 00:43:54.380
like IoT Core or Pub/Sub.

00:43:54.380 --> 00:43:57.590
Other services, like Bigtable,
if you want more throughput,

00:43:57.590 --> 00:44:00.020
more performance, you
actually do pay more

00:44:00.020 --> 00:44:01.280
for more throughput.

00:44:01.280 --> 00:44:05.900
So there is a correlation there
between cost and performance.

00:44:05.900 --> 00:44:07.730
Memory is an expensive thing.

00:44:07.730 --> 00:44:10.130
If you want to have the
performance of Redis

00:44:10.130 --> 00:44:12.230
for that high-speed
counter, you need

00:44:12.230 --> 00:44:16.640
to essentially have RAM on hand
for those kind of operations.

00:44:16.640 --> 00:44:20.342
So in general, you
need to be optimizing

00:44:20.342 --> 00:44:21.800
not only for
performance and cost--

00:44:21.800 --> 00:44:24.740
and you should be looking at
how the impact of increasing

00:44:24.740 --> 00:44:27.590
performance increases your cost
or where you get performance

00:44:27.590 --> 00:44:30.770
essentially for free that's
built into the pricing model.

00:44:35.590 --> 00:44:40.770
So in conclusion, I think be
mindful of where performance

00:44:40.770 --> 00:44:42.480
actually has impact.

00:44:42.480 --> 00:44:45.960
The phrase "premature
optimization" is real.

00:44:45.960 --> 00:44:49.800
If you're worried about
20 milliseconds somewhere

00:44:49.800 --> 00:44:55.020
on a publishing speed but
you have a transmit time

00:44:55.020 --> 00:44:58.470
of 800 milliseconds
over a satellite link,

00:44:58.470 --> 00:45:00.467
is that your biggest
concern at the moment?

00:45:00.467 --> 00:45:03.050
Should you be really trying to
shave two or three milliseconds

00:45:03.050 --> 00:45:05.770
or microseconds off of
something on the device

00:45:05.770 --> 00:45:08.490
if it's really just
a rounding error

00:45:08.490 --> 00:45:11.220
in your end-to-end latency?

00:45:11.220 --> 00:45:12.990
So consider each
of these layers.

00:45:12.990 --> 00:45:15.660
So just to review-- the
physical layer, what

00:45:15.660 --> 00:45:18.300
is the-- if you look at
some boards, for example,

00:45:18.300 --> 00:45:19.330
I mentioned temperature.

00:45:19.330 --> 00:45:21.420
Do you need to isolate the
temperature sensor away

00:45:21.420 --> 00:45:24.240
from the board so that it's
more responsive to changes

00:45:24.240 --> 00:45:25.910
in temperature?

00:45:25.910 --> 00:45:29.280
On the device, do you need
more compute or less compute?

00:45:29.280 --> 00:45:32.300
Can you get away with an
eight-bit microprocessor,

00:45:32.300 --> 00:45:33.800
or do you need
something like a CPU?

00:45:33.800 --> 00:45:35.790
Do you need Edge TPU
you on the device

00:45:35.790 --> 00:45:40.890
to do complex ML computation
on the device or not?

00:45:40.890 --> 00:45:45.411
Is the communication something
you want to pay for 5G?

00:45:45.411 --> 00:45:47.160
Do you need the
throughput, or do you just

00:45:47.160 --> 00:45:48.150
care about the latency?

00:45:48.150 --> 00:45:49.650
If you don't care
about the latency,

00:45:49.650 --> 00:45:52.710
can you deal with
something like nb-iot

00:45:52.710 --> 00:45:55.350
and just send up
hourly telemetry,

00:45:55.350 --> 00:45:56.640
and that's sufficient?

00:45:56.640 --> 00:46:00.390
So don't go and buy a
very high-throughput,

00:46:00.390 --> 00:46:02.700
high-expensive connection
if you don't actually--

00:46:02.700 --> 00:46:05.320
are not going to sort
of take advantage of it.

00:46:05.320 --> 00:46:07.050
And then on the
data side, recognize

00:46:07.050 --> 00:46:10.170
that you've got everything
from managed services,

00:46:10.170 --> 00:46:13.330
like Pub/Sub, which will be
highly performant for you to--

00:46:13.330 --> 00:46:14.820
sort of choices
you need to make.

00:46:14.820 --> 00:46:15.960
If you're going to
run a VM, you'll

00:46:15.960 --> 00:46:17.793
need to do things like
choose whether or not

00:46:17.793 --> 00:46:21.980
to use SSD or spinning disk, how
much RAM, what instance type.

00:46:21.980 --> 00:46:24.930
All of these variables on
the data processing side

00:46:24.930 --> 00:46:27.060
can impact your performance.

00:46:27.060 --> 00:46:29.850
And then on the presentation
layer on the application side,

00:46:29.850 --> 00:46:32.610
remember that when you're
dealing with humans,

00:46:32.610 --> 00:46:35.600
we can be fooled.

00:46:35.600 --> 00:46:39.150
The perception of performance is
what matters on the application

00:46:39.150 --> 00:46:39.750
side, often.

00:46:39.750 --> 00:46:43.350
And so dealing with
caching, even if it's not

00:46:43.350 --> 00:46:46.290
the freshest data, are your
users going to be happier

00:46:46.290 --> 00:46:48.810
with a zippier experience,
or do they really

00:46:48.810 --> 00:46:51.392
need the data from
one second to go?

00:46:51.392 --> 00:46:53.350
Or can you cache for five
minutes, and everyone

00:46:53.350 --> 00:46:55.740
is just happier because the
application loads instead

00:46:55.740 --> 00:46:59.640
of waiting for a bunch of back
end processes to recompute?

00:46:59.640 --> 00:47:05.460
So across the spectrum,
optimize for performance.

00:47:05.460 --> 00:47:08.340
One quick plug for those who
are still around tomorrow

00:47:08.340 --> 00:47:09.820
and wondering what to do.

00:47:09.820 --> 00:47:12.630
We have a project we've been
working on for DevOps, so this

00:47:12.630 --> 00:47:16.860
is a project for my team to
sort of make a fun game, kind

00:47:16.860 --> 00:47:20.190
of a quest and a challenge
around doing DevOps live

00:47:20.190 --> 00:47:22.460
with a bunch of
people and competing

00:47:22.460 --> 00:47:24.390
and time and challenges.

00:47:24.390 --> 00:47:28.500
So that's tomorrow afternoon
if anyone wants to do that.

00:47:28.500 --> 00:47:30.360
I'll be around for
a couple of minutes.

00:47:30.360 --> 00:47:32.900
If anyone has any questions,
I'm happy to entertain them.

00:47:32.900 --> 00:47:33.900
But thank you very much.

00:47:33.900 --> 00:47:36.300
[APPLAUSE]

00:47:36.300 --> 00:47:39.950
[MUSIC PLAYING]

